1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,200
Thirty-five dollars.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,315
That is the typical overdraft fee at

3
00:00:04,315 --> 00:00:05,834
America's largest banks,

4
00:00:05,834 --> 00:00:07,949
according to the Consumer Financial

5
00:00:07,949 --> 00:00:09,510
Protection Bureau.

6
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,409
You can pay it for spending just

7
00:00:11,409 --> 00:00:13,738
a few dollars more than you have.

8
00:00:13,983 --> 00:00:16,670
Banks have cut these fees sharply in

9
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:18,613
the last few years.

10
00:00:18,693 --> 00:00:19,711
Even so,

11
00:00:19,711 --> 00:00:21,513
in twenty twenty-three,

12
00:00:21,513 --> 00:00:23,863
banks with more than a billion dollars

13
00:00:23,863 --> 00:00:26,474
in assets still collected five point eight

14
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:29,085
three billion dollars in overdraft and

15
00:00:29,085 --> 00:00:30,990
bounced-payment fees.

16
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:34,451
So why is a fee that everyone

17
00:00:34,451 --> 00:00:37,540
seems to hate still worth billions?

18
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:39,766
And who is really paying it?

19
00:00:40,017 --> 00:00:41,902
By the end of this video,

20
00:00:41,902 --> 00:00:44,050
you will know how an overdraft fee

21
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:44,742
is made,

22
00:00:44,742 --> 00:00:46,890
why a small group of customers pays

23
00:00:46,890 --> 00:00:47,821
most of it,

24
00:00:47,821 --> 00:00:49,969
and how a federal cap on the

25
00:00:49,969 --> 00:00:52,717
fee was written and then erased.

26
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:00,750
Here is what an overdraft is.

27
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:02,500
You have twenty dollars in your checking

28
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:03,390
account.

29
00:01:03,390 --> 00:01:05,740
You buy something for twenty-five.

30
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:07,762
The bank can refuse the payment,

31
00:01:07,762 --> 00:01:09,736
or it can pay it anyway and

32
00:01:09,736 --> 00:01:12,090
push your balance below zero.

33
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:13,302
If it pays,

34
00:01:13,302 --> 00:01:15,896
it usually charges you a fee for

35
00:01:15,896 --> 00:01:17,015
doing it.

36
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:19,192
According to the bureau,

37
00:01:19,192 --> 00:01:21,895
the median overdraft fee at the largest

38
00:01:21,895 --> 00:01:23,861
institutions that charge one was

39
00:01:23,861 --> 00:01:25,690
thirty-five dollars.

40
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:27,717
The fee for a payment the bank

41
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:28,598
refuses,

42
00:01:28,598 --> 00:01:30,835
called a non-sufficient funds fee,

43
00:01:30,835 --> 00:01:32,790
was thirty-two dollars.

44
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:35,025
And the bureau found that the median

45
00:01:35,025 --> 00:01:37,319
debit card purchase that triggered an

46
00:01:37,319 --> 00:01:40,443
overdraft was only twenty-four dollars.

47
00:01:40,683 --> 00:01:43,173
Think about what that means.

48
00:01:43,193 --> 00:01:45,282
The bank covers a twenty-four dollar

49
00:01:45,282 --> 00:01:46,724
purchase for a few days,

50
00:01:46,724 --> 00:01:49,413
until your next deposit arrives.

51
00:01:49,593 --> 00:01:52,165
And it charges thirty-five dollars for the

52
00:01:52,165 --> 00:01:53,233
favor.

53
00:01:53,463 --> 00:01:55,483
If you called that a short loan,

54
00:01:55,483 --> 00:01:58,352
it would be a very expensive one.

55
00:01:58,617 --> 00:02:01,387
And the fees can stack.

56
00:02:01,467 --> 00:02:02,315
Wells Fargo,

57
00:02:02,315 --> 00:02:03,379
for example,

58
00:02:03,379 --> 00:02:05,335
still charges thirty-five dollars per

59
00:02:05,335 --> 00:02:05,966
item,

60
00:02:05,966 --> 00:02:08,087
up to three times a day.

61
00:02:08,477 --> 00:02:11,006
Three small purchases on a bad day

62
00:02:11,006 --> 00:02:13,535
can mean one hundred and five dollars

63
00:02:13,535 --> 00:02:14,696
in fees.

64
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:17,560
Let me draw a bad day.

65
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:19,680
Start at ten dollars.

66
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:20,502
A coffee,

67
00:02:20,502 --> 00:02:23,000
and the balance is still positive.

68
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:24,527
A grocery run,

69
00:02:24,527 --> 00:02:26,760
and it drops below zero.

70
00:02:26,870 --> 00:02:27,880
A fee.

71
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:28,937
A gas station,

72
00:02:28,937 --> 00:02:30,330
another fee.

73
00:02:30,330 --> 00:02:31,701
A streaming bill,

74
00:02:31,701 --> 00:02:33,140
a third fee.

75
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:35,735
The purchases add up to less than

76
00:02:35,735 --> 00:02:36,869
the fees.

77
00:02:37,117 --> 00:02:39,743
So who actually ends up paying all

78
00:02:39,743 --> 00:02:40,927
these fees?

79
00:02:41,047 --> 00:02:44,169
The answer is surprisingly concentrated.

80
00:02:54,817 --> 00:02:57,061
Most people rarely overdraft,

81
00:02:57,061 --> 00:02:58,507
or never do.

82
00:02:58,507 --> 00:03:00,732
The bureau studied millions of checking

83
00:03:00,732 --> 00:03:01,777
accounts.

84
00:03:01,907 --> 00:03:04,142
It found that about nine percent of

85
00:03:04,142 --> 00:03:06,874
accounts paid almost seventy-nine percent

86
00:03:06,874 --> 00:03:10,206
of all overdraft and bounced-payment fees.

87
00:03:10,450 --> 00:03:12,781
It gets even more extreme at the

88
00:03:12,781 --> 00:03:13,640
top.

89
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:16,196
Accounts that overdrafted more than twenty

90
00:03:16,196 --> 00:03:18,157
times a year were fewer than one

91
00:03:18,157 --> 00:03:19,410
in twenty.

92
00:03:19,730 --> 00:03:22,849
Yet they paid about sixty-three percent of

93
00:03:22,849 --> 00:03:24,300
all those fees.

94
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:25,349
For the bank,

95
00:03:25,349 --> 00:03:27,806
the overdraft business does not depend on

96
00:03:27,806 --> 00:03:29,020
everyone.

97
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:30,925
It depends on a small group of

98
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:32,665
repeat customers.

99
00:03:32,917 --> 00:03:35,549
Think about who overdraws on a twenty-four

100
00:03:35,549 --> 00:03:37,027
dollar purchase.

101
00:03:37,317 --> 00:03:40,127
Usually not someone with a large balance.

102
00:03:40,287 --> 00:03:41,146
More often,

103
00:03:41,146 --> 00:03:43,727
someone waiting for the next paycheck.

104
00:03:43,797 --> 00:03:45,710
That is why critics say the fee

105
00:03:45,710 --> 00:03:47,623
falls hardest on those who can least

106
00:03:47,623 --> 00:03:48,940
afford it.

107
00:03:49,183 --> 00:03:51,963
Some practices made it worse.

108
00:03:51,963 --> 00:03:54,174
The bureau described how some banks

109
00:03:54,174 --> 00:03:56,877
processed the largest payment of the day

110
00:03:56,877 --> 00:03:57,723
first.

111
00:03:57,763 --> 00:03:59,846
Paying the big bill first could empty

112
00:03:59,846 --> 00:04:01,381
the account faster,

113
00:04:01,381 --> 00:04:03,985
so that several small purchases each

114
00:04:03,985 --> 00:04:06,146
triggered their own fee.

115
00:04:06,383 --> 00:04:08,689
Regulators also went after something

116
00:04:08,689 --> 00:04:09,673
stranger.

117
00:04:09,673 --> 00:04:11,846
A purchase was approved when the account

118
00:04:11,846 --> 00:04:13,011
had enough money,

119
00:04:13,011 --> 00:04:14,572
but by the time it settled,

120
00:04:14,572 --> 00:04:15,935
the balance had dropped,

121
00:04:15,935 --> 00:04:17,910
and the bank charged an overdraft fee

122
00:04:17,910 --> 00:04:19,103
anyway.

123
00:04:19,243 --> 00:04:21,485
The bureau called these fees likely

124
00:04:21,485 --> 00:04:22,533
unfair.

125
00:04:22,833 --> 00:04:25,065
Its examiners found at least tens of

126
00:04:25,065 --> 00:04:26,829
millions of dollars in harm,

127
00:04:26,829 --> 00:04:28,615
and refunds went to more than one

128
00:04:28,615 --> 00:04:31,671
hundred and seventy thousand customers.

129
00:04:31,917 --> 00:04:34,327
Let me draw who pays.

130
00:04:34,347 --> 00:04:36,827
Here are one hundred checking accounts.

131
00:04:36,827 --> 00:04:37,695
Most of them,

132
00:04:37,695 --> 00:04:38,562
ninety-one,

133
00:04:38,562 --> 00:04:40,497
pay little or nothing.

134
00:04:40,587 --> 00:04:42,737
Nine accounts are on the other side.

135
00:04:42,737 --> 00:04:44,761
And those nine pay almost four out

136
00:04:44,761 --> 00:04:47,385
of every five fee dollars.

137
00:04:47,650 --> 00:04:48,378
For years,

138
00:04:48,378 --> 00:04:51,490
that model was very profitable.

139
00:04:52,110 --> 00:04:52,511
Then,

140
00:04:52,511 --> 00:04:53,758
almost overnight,

141
00:04:53,758 --> 00:04:55,870
the biggest banks started backing away

142
00:04:55,870 --> 00:04:56,892
from it.

143
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:05,840
The first crack came years earlier.

144
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:07,034
In twenty ten,

145
00:05:07,034 --> 00:05:10,000
a Federal Reserve rule took effect.

146
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:12,371
Banks could no longer charge overdraft

147
00:05:12,371 --> 00:05:15,320
fees on ATM withdrawals or one-time debit

148
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:18,023
card purchases unless the customer had

149
00:05:18,023 --> 00:05:19,360
opted in.

150
00:05:19,590 --> 00:05:22,910
Customers had to say yes first.

151
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:25,166
It was the first time the default

152
00:05:25,166 --> 00:05:26,533
had changed.

153
00:05:26,783 --> 00:05:28,771
The bigger shift came at the end

154
00:05:28,771 --> 00:05:30,473
of twenty twenty-one.

155
00:05:30,473 --> 00:05:32,813
Capital One announced it would stop

156
00:05:32,813 --> 00:05:35,153
charging overdraft and bounced-payment

157
00:05:35,153 --> 00:05:36,923
fees altogether.

158
00:05:37,163 --> 00:05:38,114
Weeks later,

159
00:05:38,114 --> 00:05:40,551
Wells Fargo dropped its bounced-payment

160
00:05:40,551 --> 00:05:42,745
fee and added a grace period of

161
00:05:42,745 --> 00:05:43,939
twenty-four hours,

162
00:05:43,939 --> 00:05:46,976
though it kept the overdraft fee itself.

163
00:05:47,217 --> 00:05:49,414
In February twenty twenty-two,

164
00:05:49,414 --> 00:05:51,634
Citi said it would end overdraft fees

165
00:05:51,634 --> 00:05:52,722
completely,

166
00:05:52,722 --> 00:05:54,941
and called itself the only top five

167
00:05:54,941 --> 00:05:57,317
American bank to do so.

168
00:05:57,547 --> 00:06:00,083
Chase added a fifty-dollar cushion before

169
00:06:00,083 --> 00:06:01,317
any fee.

170
00:06:01,617 --> 00:06:04,208
And Bank of America reported that its

171
00:06:04,208 --> 00:06:06,150
service charges fell by one point one

172
00:06:06,150 --> 00:06:07,856
billion dollars in one year,

173
00:06:07,856 --> 00:06:10,015
mainly because of its new overdraft

174
00:06:10,015 --> 00:06:11,262
policies.

175
00:06:11,517 --> 00:06:13,422
Across the industry,

176
00:06:13,422 --> 00:06:14,967
the drop was huge.

177
00:06:14,967 --> 00:06:17,050
The bureau counted overdraft and

178
00:06:17,050 --> 00:06:19,364
bounced-payment revenue at banks with more

179
00:06:19,364 --> 00:06:22,047
than a billion dollars in assets.

180
00:06:22,157 --> 00:06:24,329
It was eleven point nine six billion

181
00:06:24,329 --> 00:06:26,667
dollars in twenty nineteen.

182
00:06:26,807 --> 00:06:28,266
By twenty twenty-three,

183
00:06:28,266 --> 00:06:29,957
it was five point eight three billion

184
00:06:29,957 --> 00:06:30,570
dollars,

185
00:06:30,570 --> 00:06:31,867
down fifty-one percent.

186
00:06:31,867 --> 00:06:34,641
The bureau estimated that the average

187
00:06:34,641 --> 00:06:37,415
overdrafting household saved about one

188
00:06:37,415 --> 00:06:40,326
hundred and eighty-five dollars a year.

189
00:06:40,583 --> 00:06:42,793
Let me draw the drop.

190
00:06:42,883 --> 00:06:44,879
Here is twenty nineteen,

191
00:06:44,879 --> 00:06:45,864
a tall bar,

192
00:06:45,864 --> 00:06:48,233
almost twelve billion dollars.

193
00:06:48,313 --> 00:06:50,193
Here is twenty twenty-three,

194
00:06:50,193 --> 00:06:51,973
less than half as tall.

195
00:06:51,973 --> 00:06:53,513
The banks gave up more than six

196
00:06:53,513 --> 00:06:55,433
billion dollars a year.

197
00:06:55,473 --> 00:06:57,233
But look at what is left.

198
00:06:57,233 --> 00:06:59,517
Almost six billion dollars a year is

199
00:06:59,517 --> 00:07:01,945
still a very big business.

200
00:07:02,183 --> 00:07:05,393
So regulators decided to go further.

201
00:07:05,403 --> 00:07:06,779
And for a few months,

202
00:07:06,779 --> 00:07:09,344
it looked like the thirty-five dollar fee

203
00:07:09,344 --> 00:07:10,877
might disappear.

204
00:07:16,517 --> 00:07:19,064
In December twenty twenty-four,

205
00:07:19,064 --> 00:07:21,894
the bureau issued a final rule for

206
00:07:21,894 --> 00:07:24,209
banks and credit unions with more than

207
00:07:24,209 --> 00:07:26,867
ten billion dollars in assets.

208
00:07:26,997 --> 00:07:29,187
They would have three choices.

209
00:07:29,227 --> 00:07:31,484
Charge an overdraft fee of no more

210
00:07:31,484 --> 00:07:33,087
than five dollars.

211
00:07:33,147 --> 00:07:35,380
Charge a fee that only covered their

212
00:07:35,380 --> 00:07:37,097
own costs and losses.

213
00:07:37,247 --> 00:07:39,506
Or treat the overdraft as a loan,

214
00:07:39,506 --> 00:07:41,766
under the federal lending law,

215
00:07:41,766 --> 00:07:43,820
with the disclosures that come with

216
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:44,876
credit.

217
00:07:45,117 --> 00:07:47,498
The bureau projected that the rule could

218
00:07:47,498 --> 00:07:49,880
save consumers up to five billion dollars

219
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:50,508
a year,

220
00:07:50,508 --> 00:07:52,673
or about two hundred and twenty-five

221
00:07:52,673 --> 00:07:54,621
dollars for each household that pays

222
00:07:54,621 --> 00:07:56,087
overdraft fees.

223
00:07:56,327 --> 00:07:57,752
It was due to take effect in

224
00:07:57,752 --> 00:07:59,777
October twenty twenty-five.

225
00:07:59,937 --> 00:08:02,143
Wells Fargo warned its investors that it

226
00:08:02,143 --> 00:08:04,550
would expect a significant reduction in

227
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:06,153
its overdraft fees.

228
00:08:06,417 --> 00:08:08,097
It never took effect.

229
00:08:08,097 --> 00:08:09,510
In twenty twenty-five,

230
00:08:09,510 --> 00:08:11,354
Congress used a law called the

231
00:08:11,354 --> 00:08:14,207
Congressional Review Act to cancel it.

232
00:08:14,407 --> 00:08:16,407
The Senate voted in March.

233
00:08:16,407 --> 00:08:18,452
The House voted two hundred and seventeen

234
00:08:18,452 --> 00:08:21,098
to two hundred and eleven in April.

235
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:22,632
And on May ninth,

236
00:08:22,632 --> 00:08:24,176
twenty twenty-five,

237
00:08:24,176 --> 00:08:26,820
the resolution became law.

238
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:28,960
Its text is very short.

239
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:29,935
The rule,

240
00:08:29,935 --> 00:08:30,646
it says,

241
00:08:30,646 --> 00:08:32,940
shall have no force or effect.

242
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:35,586
The five-dollar option was gone before a

243
00:08:35,586 --> 00:08:37,950
single bank had to offer it.

244
00:08:38,183 --> 00:08:40,703
Let me draw the timeline.

245
00:08:40,773 --> 00:08:42,553
December twenty twenty-four,

246
00:08:42,553 --> 00:08:44,353
the rule is written.

247
00:08:44,533 --> 00:08:46,293
October twenty twenty-five,

248
00:08:46,293 --> 00:08:48,673
the rule was supposed to start.

249
00:08:48,863 --> 00:08:50,085
But in between,

250
00:08:50,085 --> 00:08:52,056
in May twenty twenty-five,

251
00:08:52,056 --> 00:08:54,153
Congress erases it.

252
00:08:54,283 --> 00:08:56,616
The fee cap lived for five months,

253
00:08:56,616 --> 00:08:58,527
only on paper.

254
00:08:58,783 --> 00:09:01,279
So with no federal cap,

255
00:09:01,279 --> 00:09:04,407
what happened to the fees next?

256
00:09:10,050 --> 00:09:12,450
The bureau has not published a new

257
00:09:12,450 --> 00:09:14,970
total since twenty twenty-three.

258
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:18,102
But outside researchers read the same bank

259
00:09:18,102 --> 00:09:19,270
reports.

260
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:22,348
The consulting firm Bates White estimates

261
00:09:22,348 --> 00:09:24,306
that banks with more than a billion

262
00:09:24,306 --> 00:09:26,999
dollars in assets collected about six

263
00:09:26,999 --> 00:09:29,202
point one billion dollars in twenty

264
00:09:29,202 --> 00:09:30,157
twenty-four,

265
00:09:30,157 --> 00:09:32,360
about three percent more than the year

266
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:33,450
before.

267
00:09:33,650 --> 00:09:34,763
In its words,

268
00:09:34,763 --> 00:09:38,217
the steep decline appears to have ended.

269
00:09:38,483 --> 00:09:41,333
The money is concentrated at the top.

270
00:09:41,333 --> 00:09:44,024
The Consumer Federation of America,

271
00:09:44,024 --> 00:09:45,359
an advocacy group,

272
00:09:45,359 --> 00:09:46,908
counted four point eight eight billion

273
00:09:46,908 --> 00:09:48,844
dollars in twenty twenty-four at banks

274
00:09:48,844 --> 00:09:50,586
with more than ten billion dollars in

275
00:09:50,586 --> 00:09:51,573
assets.

276
00:09:51,993 --> 00:09:54,292
JPMorgan Chase alone took about one

277
00:09:54,292 --> 00:09:55,153
billion.

278
00:09:55,153 --> 00:09:57,502
Wells Fargo was close behind.

279
00:09:57,750 --> 00:10:00,880
And those numbers leave out a lot.

280
00:10:01,110 --> 00:10:03,489
Credit unions and smaller banks do not

281
00:10:03,489 --> 00:10:05,040
appear in them.

282
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:06,696
Counting everyone,

283
00:10:06,696 --> 00:10:09,684
the National Consumer Law Center cites an

284
00:10:09,684 --> 00:10:12,213
estimate of about twelve point one billion

285
00:10:12,213 --> 00:10:14,741
dollars in overdraft and bounced-payment

286
00:10:14,741 --> 00:10:16,950
fees in twenty twenty-four.

287
00:10:17,190 --> 00:10:18,617
That is an estimate,

288
00:10:18,617 --> 00:10:20,685
and it measures a much wider group

289
00:10:20,685 --> 00:10:22,319
