290
00:10:22,583 --> 00:10:24,943
Why does the fee survive?

291
00:10:25,123 --> 00:10:27,173
Look at how it is built.

292
00:10:27,483 --> 00:10:29,323
It is a flat fee.

293
00:10:29,633 --> 00:10:31,753
At a bank like Wells Fargo,

294
00:10:31,753 --> 00:10:34,705
it is the same thirty-five dollars whether

295
00:10:34,705 --> 00:10:37,388
you overdraw by two dollars or by

296
00:10:37,388 --> 00:10:38,793
two hundred.

297
00:10:39,123 --> 00:10:40,416
On a small purchase,

298
00:10:40,416 --> 00:10:42,607
covered for a few days until the

299
00:10:42,607 --> 00:10:43,680
next deposit,

300
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:46,033
that is a very wide margin.

301
00:10:46,233 --> 00:10:48,521
And without a federal cap,

302
00:10:48,521 --> 00:10:51,091
each bank still decides for itself what

303
00:10:51,091 --> 00:10:52,205
to charge.

304
00:10:52,450 --> 00:10:55,600
Let me draw the overdraft machine.

305
00:10:55,700 --> 00:10:56,601
On the left,

306
00:10:56,601 --> 00:10:59,050
a customer close to zero.

307
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:00,353
In the middle,

308
00:11:00,353 --> 00:11:02,600
the bank paying a small purchase.

309
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:03,555
On the right,

310
00:11:03,555 --> 00:11:04,235
the fee,

311
00:11:04,235 --> 00:11:07,180
coming back the moment the paycheck lands.

312
00:11:07,270 --> 00:11:08,574
Then the next month,

313
00:11:08,574 --> 00:11:10,144
the same customer,

314
00:11:10,144 --> 00:11:11,542
the same loop.

315
00:11:11,783 --> 00:11:13,996
So why do banks still make billions

316
00:11:13,996 --> 00:11:15,703
from overdrafts?

317
00:11:16,033 --> 00:11:17,110
Because the fee is small for most

318
00:11:17,110 --> 00:11:18,343
people and huge for a few.

319
00:11:18,343 --> 00:11:20,760
It is charged on tiny purchases,

320
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:22,756
paid by customers who live close to

321
00:11:22,756 --> 00:11:23,399
zero,

322
00:11:23,399 --> 00:11:25,753
and repeated month after month.

323
00:11:25,753 --> 00:11:26,841
Banks cut it in half when the

324
00:11:26,841 --> 00:11:28,063
pressure was high.

325
00:11:28,273 --> 00:11:30,734
The cap that would have cut it

326
00:11:30,734 --> 00:11:33,073
again was erased.

327
00:11:33,073 --> 00:11:34,969
If you want to keep your money

328
00:11:34,969 --> 00:11:36,367
out of that machine,

329
00:11:36,367 --> 00:11:38,500
the simplest move is to check whether

330
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:40,048
you opted in.

331
00:11:40,283 --> 00:11:42,914
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

332
00:11:42,914 --> 00:11:46,052
we follow the money behind another hidden

333
00:11:46,052 --> 00:11:48,563
link of the world economy.

334
00:11:48,563 --> 00:11:51,020
This is The Hidden Economics.
